members.member:
If [code]true[/code], a page that embeds the game in an [code]<iframe>[/code], including one on another origin, can put it in fullscreen with its own button, and the game sees a normal [constant DisplayServer.WINDOW_MODE_FULLSCREEN] at the screen's full resolution. A click in the parent page does not count as user activation inside a cross-origin iframe, so that page cannot put the game's canvas in fullscreen; it fullscreens the iframe instead and tells the game with [code]postMessage()[/code]:
- parent to game, [code]{type: 'godot:fullscreen', on: true}[/code] (or [code]false[/code]): sent on every [code]fullscreenchange[/code] in the parent, and in answer to [code]godot:ready[/code];
- game to parent, [code]{type: 'godot:ready'}[/code]: the engine has started and listens, so a fullscreen entered on the start screen or while loading is picked up;
- game to parent, [code]{type: 'godot:exit-fullscreen'}[/code]: [method DisplayServer.window_set_mode] was called with [constant DisplayServer.WINDOW_MODE_WINDOWED] while the parent holds the fullscreen. The mode changes once the parent reports that it left;
- game to parent, [code]{type: 'godot:request-fullscreen'}[/code]: the game asked for fullscreen. If the parent does not answer within a second, the game requests fullscreen for its canvas instead, as without this option.
Only messages from the parent window count, and only from [member html/embed_fullscreen/allowed_origins] when it is set. The parent's [code]<iframe>[/code] needs [code]allow="fullscreen"[/code]. The game's own fullscreen keeps working as before. Without this option, no message listener is added. A custom HTML shell receives these settings as [code]embedFullscreen[/code] in [code]$GODOT_CONFIG[/code]. Default: [code]false[/code].
